# The std library's ROOT expression (design/caos-expr.md, "Deep deps"): std is
# published UN-deepened — every entry carries the `DEPS` file it was checked in
# with — and this expression is what turns that into the deepened tree callers
# resolve against. `eval-path` into `std/<name>` applies this first, so an
# entry's `DEEP-DEPS/<dep>` mounts are computed by the REAL deep-deps worker at
# resolve time instead of being baked in by bootstrap. That is what makes the
# checked-in tree self-resolving: an entry is reached BY DESCENT through this
# expression, never by a name looked up against a pre-deepened ref.
#
# `deep-deps` names the sibling entry BY PATH, and that is what breaks cycle-2
# (design/caos-expr.md, "Breaking the cycles"): `resolve_expr_image` evaluates
# the named subtree against ITSELF, so it sees `std/deep-deps`'s own
# `.caos-expr` — the `docker://seeded-deep-deps` sentinel the core-seeder-runner
# answers — and never re-enters this root expression. Nothing here needs
# deep-deps to already be deepened, because the seeded entry has no DEPS.
#
# bootstrap (`build-builtins.sh`) still hand-deepens, but ONLY to form the seed
# keys: a seeded item's `in` is its DEEPENED entry, i.e. what this expression
# produces. So the hand-deepen and this worker must agree BYTE FOR BYTE or the
# seeder's key misses — see `deepen_entry` there.
run deep-deps -- --in:@=.
